 Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs // Restore the saved color scheme preference, or // enact the browser preference if "automatic", // without expecting DOM load to have completed. // // Also restore any saved readingmode and ToC display preferences. function initializeReadingPreferences() { let saved_theme = localStorage.getItem("ar5iv_theme") || "automatic"; if (saved_theme === "automatic") { if (window.matchMedia("(prefers-color-scheme: dark)").matches) { saved_theme = "dark"; } } if (saved_theme == "dark") { document.documentElement.setAttribute("data-theme", "dark"); } else { document.documentElement.setAttribute("data-theme", "light"); } const tocDisplay = localStorage.getItem('arxiv_html_paper_toc_display'); if (tocDisplay) { document.documentElement.setAttribute("data-toc-display", tocDisplay); } const readingMode = localStorage.getItem('arxiv_html_paper_reading_mode'); if (readingMode) { document.documentElement.setAttribute("data-reading-mode", readingMode); } } // Run as soon as JS starts, to minimize repainting initializeReadingPreferences(); /* Reading mode hides the chrome and the empty .arxiv-html-header marker: the pinned JS reads the marker's display to know which way to toggle. The marker draws no box. */ .arxiv-html-header { display: contents; } html[data-reading-mode="enabled"] :is(.arxiv-html-site-chrome, .arxiv-html-header, .ds-site-footer) { display: none; } /* The paper controls' icons are blocks, so they sit centred (the pinned JS shows the theme icon by making its span a block). */ .arxiv-html-site-chrome .ds-site-header-tools svg { display: block; width: 18px; height: 18px; flex-shrink: 0; fill: currentColor; } /* ar5iv dims every image in dark mode and puts it on a white wash, for paper figures; arXiv's own render as they are (the logo on the bar, the funder logos on their white chips). Important: ar5iv's dark rules are unlayered, as specific as :root:not([data-theme]) img:not(.ltx_no_dark_filter). */ :is(.ds-site-header, .ds-site-footer) img { filter: none !important; } .ds-site-header img { background-color: transparent !important; } /* Wide (the theme's 1280px): the theme lays the body out as a grid whose full-width top rows were the band's and its sticky reader bar's. The band takes the first and scrolls away; the header, the paper's bar (no site navigation, the paper's controls), takes the second and stays in view, the one sticky bar, with the anchor clearance that requires (DESIGN-POLICIES). The controls show their names. */ @media (min-width: 1280px) { html { scroll-padding-top: 80px; } .arxiv-html-site-chrome { display: contents; } .arxiv-html-site-chrome > #ds-announcement { grid-area: announce; } .arxiv-html-site-chrome > .ds-site-header { grid-area: header; position: sticky; top: 0; z-index: 3; } .arxiv-html-site-chrome .tool-icon { display: none; } } /* Narrow: the header scrolls away, and the controls show icons (names kept for screen readers; Why HTML? goes). The theme's contents panel is fixed below where its old sticky bar was, and hidden only once the reader has closed it, so until then it is an invisible box over the header and the paper: it shows only while open, under the chrome, in its place in the page. The paper's footer (build logs, reporting instructions) keeps the text's side inset; the theme gives it none, so it met the screen edges. */ @media (max-width: 1279px) { .arxiv-html-site-chrome .tool-wide { display: none; } .arxiv-html-site-chrome .tool-label { position: absolute; width: 1px; height: 1px; overflow: hidden; clip: rect(0 0 0 0); white-space: nowrap; } html:not([data-toc-display="block"]) .ltx_page_navbar { display: none; } .ltx_page_navbar { position: absolute; top: auto; } .arxiv-html-footer { padding-inline: 1rem; } } Skip to main content Report GitHub Issue × Title: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below: Description: Submit without GitHub Submit in GitHub Why HTML? Report Issue Back to Abstract Download PDF Contents Reading mode Colour theme Abstract. 1 Introduction 1.1 Connections to local-global theory 2 Background 2.1 Locally chordal graphs 2.2 Interval graphs 2.3 The local covering 3 Proof of Theorem 1 4 Characterization of 3-locally chordal graphs References License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license arXiv:2512.19040v1 [math.CO] 22 Dec 2025 Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs Thanks: T.A. is supported by the National Science Foundation Award Number DMS-2303251 and the Alexander von Humboldt Foundation. S.A. is supported by the Alexander von Humboldt Foundation in the framework of the Alexander von Humboldt Professorship of Daniel Král’ endowed by the Federal Ministry of Education and Research. P.K. is supported by a doctoral scholarship of the Studienstiftung des deutschen Volkes. Tara Abrishami, Sandra Albrechtsen, Nathan Bowler, Paul Knappe, Jana Katharina Nickel Address: Stanford University, Department of Mathematics Email address: tara.abrishami@stanford.edu Address: Leipzig University, Institute of Mathematics Email address: sandra@albrechtsen-mail.de Address: University of Hamburg, Department of Mathematics Email address: {nathan.bowler, paul.knappe, jana.nickel-2}@uni-hamburg.de Abstract. Circular-arc graphs are graphs that can be represented as intersection graphs of subpaths of a cycle. Interval graphs are graphs that can be represented as intersection graphs of subpaths of a path. Since cycles are locally paths, every circular-arc graph is locally interval. In this paper, we prove that the converse holds as well: every locally interval graph is a circular-arc graph. This result and its proofs are connected to a recent broader study of structural local-global theory and build on previous work on locally chordal graphs. Key words and phrases: Locally interval, interval, chordal, local covering 1. Introduction If every vertex in a connected graph has degree at most two, then the graph is a path or a cycle. This observation can be extended: if the radius- r r ball around every vertex of a connected graph G G is a path, then the graph G G is either a path or a cycle of length greater than 2 ​ r 2r . Since small-radius balls in a long cycle are all paths, this is a characterization: a graph is a path or a long cycle if and only if every small-radius ball of the graph is a path. In this paper, we take this simple idea — that paths and long cycles are precisely the shapes that are locally paths everywhere — and apply it to a richer class of graphs. Roughly, we prove that the graphs that are locally “path-like” everywhere are precisely the graphs that are globally “path-like” or “long cycle-like.” To make this precise, let us give some definitions. A graph G G is an interval graph if there is a path P P and a mapping v ↦ P v v\mapsto P_{v} from vertices v v of G G to subpaths P v P_{v} of P P such that there is an edge u ​ v uv in G G if and only if P u P_{u} and P v P_{v} intersect in at least one vertex. We call such a mapping v ↦ P v v\mapsto P_{v} an interval representation (of G G over P P ) . Interval graphs are a special case of more general graphs, region intersection graphs. A graph G G is a region intersection graph over H H if there is a mapping v ↦ H v v\mapsto H_{v} from vertices v v of G G to connected subgraphs (i.e. regions ) H v H_{v} of H H such that u ​ v uv is an edge of G G if and only if H u H_{u} and H v H_{v} intersect in at least one vertex. The mapping v ↦ H v v\mapsto H_{v} is called a region representation (of G G over H H ) . In this language, interval graphs are the class of graphs which admit region representations over paths. Because of their characterization as precisely the graphs which admit region representations over paths, interval graphs can be reasonably understood as graphs which are “path-like.” We are interested in graphs which are “locally path-like,” so let us next understand what we mean by “locally.” Given a graph G G and a vertex v v of G G , the ball of radius r / 2 r/2 around v v , denoted B G ​ ( v , r / 2 ) B_{G}(v,r/2) , is the subgraph of G G with vertex set consisting of the vertices of distance at most ⌊ r / 2 ⌋ \lfloor r/2\rfloor from v v , and edge set consisting of the edges x ​ y xy of G G such that d ⁡ ( v , x ) + d ⁡ ( v , y ) < r d(v,x)+d(v,y)<r . A graph G G is r r -locally interval for an integer r ⩾ 0 r\geqslant 0 if all its r / 2 r/2 -balls B G ​ ( v , r / 2 ) B_{G}(v,r/2) are interval graphs. What kind of graphs might be locally interval? Since every interval graph has a region representation over a path, it seems sensible to look for graphs that have a region representation over a graph that is “locally a path.” Therefore, a good guess seems to be that locally interval graphs are exactly those graphs which have region representations over a (long) cycle. These form a well-known graph class, the circular-arc graphs. A graph G G is circular-arc if it has a region representation v ↦ C v v\mapsto C_{v} over a cycle C C such that the regions C v C_{v} are paths. We refer to such region representations over cycles as circular-arc representations . A circular-arc representation v ↦ C v v\mapsto C_{v} of a graph G G over a cycle C C is r r -acyclic if for every vertex-set X ⊆ V ⁡ ( G ) X\subseteq V(G) of size at most r r , the subgraph ⋃ x ∈ X C x \bigcup_{x\in X}C_{x} of C C is acyclic. We call a graph G G an r r -acyclic circular-arc graph if G G has an r r -acyclic circular-arc representation. Note that ∞ \infty -acyclic circular-arc graphs are precisely interval graphs, because ⋃ v ∈ V ⁡ ( G ) C v \bigcup_{v\in V(G)}C_{v} is then a path. With these definitions, the following intuitive statement, conjectured by Paweł Rzążewski in private communication, seems morally true: Conjecture 1.1 (Rzążewski 2024) . Locally interval graphs are exactly circular-arc graphs. We make this conjecture precise via our definitions above. Specifically, we ask: are the r r -locally interval graphs precisely the r r -acyclic circular-arc graphs? It turns out that this is false for r ⩽ 3 r\leqslant 3 , but we prove that it is true for r ⩾ 4 r\geqslant 4 : Theorem 1.2 . Let G G be a finite connected graph and r ⩾ 4 r\geqslant 4 an integer. Then, G G is r r -locally interval if and only if G G is r r -acyclic circular-arc. Circular-arc graphs are widely studied. Recently, Cao, Grippo, and Safe [ 3 ] gave a forbidden induced subgraph characterization of 3-acyclic circular-arc graphs; see Theorem 3.1 . Building on their work, we give a forbidden induced subgraph characterization of r r -locally interval graphs for r ⩾ 3 r\geqslant 3 . 1.1. Connections to local-global theory In addition to addressing an intuitive and visually appealing idea, our study of locally interval graphs connects to a broader study of local-global graph theory. This line of study began with the work of Diestel, Jacobs, Knappe, and Kurkofka [ 4 ] , which introduced an idea called the r r -local covering to study the “ r r -local” structure of a graph. Given a graph G G and an integer r ⩾ 1 r\geqslant 1 , the r r -local covering of G G , denoted p r : G r → G p_{r}:G_{r}\to G , is the universal covering of G G that preserves its r / 2 r/2 -balls. (See Section 2.3 for the formal definition.) The graph G r G_{r} is called the r r -local cover of G G . In [ 1 , 2 ] , the first and fourth authors and Kobler studied locally chordal graphs . A graph is chordal if each of its cycles of length at least four has a chord. Equivalently, a graph G G is chordal if and only if G G admits a region representation over a tree. Since interval graphs are the graphs that admit region representations over paths, interval graphs are a subclass of chordal graphs. A graph is r r -locally chordal if each of its r / 2 r/2 -balls is chordal. The following theorem characterizes locally chordal graphs by their r r -local covers, by forbidden induced subgraphs, and by region representations. Formal definitions for statements (iii) and (iv) below can be found in Section 2.1 . Theorem 1.3 ( [ 1 ] , Theorem 1 and [ 2 ] , Theorem 1) . Let G G be a graph and let r ⩾ 3 r\geqslant 3 be an integer. The following are equivalent: (i) G G is r r -locally chordal. (ii) The r r -local cover G r G_{r} of G G is chordal. (iii) G G is r r -chordal and wheel-free. (iv) G G is an r r -acyclic region intersection graph. Our main result in this paper is an analogous theorem for locally interval graphs, giving characterizations by their local covers, by forbidden induced subgraphs, and as circular-arc graphs. Theorem 1 . Let G G be a finite connected graph and r ⩾ 4 r\geqslant 4 an integer. Then the following are equivalent: (i) G G is r r -locally interval. (ii) The r r -local cover G r G_{r} of G G is interval. (iii) G G is r r -chordal, wheel-free, and contains none of the graphs depicted in Figure 1 as an induced subgraph. If r = 4 r=4 , then G G also contains none of the graphs depicted in Figure 3 as an induced subgraph. (iv) G G is r r -acyclic circular-arc. 2. Background 2.1. Locally chordal graphs We begin by giving the definitions necessary to understand statements (iii) and (iv) of Theorem 1.3 . A graph is r r -chordal if each of its cycles of length at least four and at most r r has a chord. Equivalently, a graph is r r -chordal if it does not contain an induced cycle of length ℓ \ell with 4 ⩽ ℓ ⩽ r 4\leqslant\ell\leqslant r . A wheel W n W_{n} for n ⩾ 4 n\geqslant 4 is a graph consisting of a cycle C n C_{n} of length n n and an additional vertex v v that is adjacent to every vertex of C n C_{n} . A graph is wheel-free if it does not contain a wheel W n W_{n} for n ⩾ 4 n\geqslant 4 as an induced subgraph. Observe that every chordal graph is r r -chordal and wheel-free. The equivalence between (i) and (iii) in Theorem 1.3 states that being r r -chordal and wheel-free is equivalent to being r r -locally chordal. A region of a graph G G is a connected subgraph of G G . A family of regions 𝐇 \mathbf{H} of a graph H H is Helly if it satisfies the Helly property : every finite subset of pairwise intersecting regions of 𝐇 \mathbf{H} has nonempty intersection. A region representation v ↦ H v v\mapsto H_{v} of a graph G G over a graph H H is r r -acyclic if for every set X ⊆ V ⁡ ( G ) X\subseteq V(G) of size at most r r , the subgraph ⋃ x ∈ X H x \bigcup_{x\in X}H_{x} of H H is acyclic. A graph G G is an r r -acyclic region intersection graph if G G admits an r r -acyclic region representation. Next, we give two statements from [ 2 ] that we will use in the proof of our main result. Lemma 2.1 ( [ 2 ] , Lemma 3.6) . Let 𝐇 \mathbf{H} be a family of regions of a (possibly infinite) graph H H . Then 𝐇 \mathbf{H} is 3-acyclic if and only if 𝐇 \mathbf{H} is 2-acyclic and Helly. Theorem 2.2 ( [ 2 ] , Theorem 5) . Let v ↦ H v v\mapsto H_{v} be a 3-acyclic region representation of a (possibly infinite) graph G G over a (possibly infinite) graph H H and let r > 3 r>3 be an integer. Then, G G is r r -locally chordal if and only if v ↦ H v v\mapsto H_{v} is r r -acyclic. 2.2. Interval graphs Boland & Lekkeikerker [ 6 ] characterized finite interval graphs by forbidden induced subgraphs: Theorem 2.3 (Boland & Lekkeikerker [ 6 ] , II) . Let G G be a (possibly infinite) 1 1 1 Even though Boland and Lekkeikerker prove Theorem 2.3 only for finite graphs, Halin [ 5 , (8)] showed that an infinite graph is interval if and only if its finite induced subgraphs are interval, which implies Theorem 2.3 for infinite graphs. graph. Then the following are equivalent: (i) G G is interval. (ii) G G is chordal and contains none of the graphs depicted in Figure 1 as an induced subgraph. (a) Long claw (b) Whipping top (c) † {\dagger} (d) ‡ {\ddagger} Figure 1 . Chordal minimal forbidden induced subgraphs of interval graphs. The dotted lines in Figure 1c and Figure 1d represent paths of arbitrary length, except one of the dotted lines in Figure 1c must be of length at least one. As a sanity check, we show that every interval graph is indeed locally interval. (Observe that this is not immediate from the definition. Every induced subgraph of an interval graph is an interval graph, but when r r is even, the r / 2 r/2 -balls of a graph are not necessarily induced subgraphs.) Lemma 2.4 . Every (possibly infinite) interval graph is r r -locally interval for every integer r ⩾ 0 r\geqslant 0 . Proof. Let G G be an interval graph and r ⩾ 0 r\geqslant 0 an integer. Since interval graphs are chordal, the graph G G is chordal, and so in particular, G G is r r -chordal and wheel-free. Therefore, by Theorem 1.3 , G G is r r -locally chordal. Suppose for a contradiction that an r / 2 r/2 -ball B B in G G around a vertex v v contains a graph F F depicted in Figure 1 as induced subgraph. If r r is odd, then B B is an induced subgraph of G G , and thus the non-interval graph F F is also an induced subgraph of G G . Therefore, assume that r r is even, and set d ≔ r / 2 d\coloneqq r/2 . Since F F is not an induced subgraph of G G , some two vertices of F F have to lie in N d ​ ( v ) N^{d}(v) , where N d ​ ( v ) N^{d}(v) denotes the set of vertices of distance exactly d d from v v . By Theorem 1.3 , for every vertex u ∈ N d ​ ( v ) u\in N^{d}(v) , its neighborhood N B ​ ( u ) N_{B}(u) is a clique in B B . Thus, only the red circled vertices in Figure 1 may be contained in N d ​ ( v ) N^{d}(v) . It is a simple check that every potential edge of G G between some pair of red circled vertices yields an induced cycle of length at least 4 4 in G G , which contradicts that G G is chordal. ∎ 2.3. The local covering Finally, we introduce the r r -local covering. A covering of a connected graph G G is a surjective mapping p : C → G p:C\to G from a graph C C to G G such that for every vertex c c of C C , the mapping p p restricts to an isomorphism from the edges incident to c c (in C C ) to the edges incident to p ⁡ ( c ) p(c) (in G G ). Given an integer r ⩾ 1 r\geqslant 1 , a covering p : C → G p:C\to G is r / 2 r/2 -ball-preserving if p p restricts to an isomorphism from B G ​ ( c , r / 2 ) B_{G}(c,r/2) to B G ​ ( p ​ ( c ) , r / 2 ) B_{G}(p(c),r/2) for every vertex c c of C C . A covering p : C → G p:C\to G of G G is universal among a set of coverings 𝒞 \mathcal{C} of G G if for every covering q : D → G q:D\to G in 𝒞 \mathcal{C} there exists a covering s : C → D s:C\to D such that p = q ∘ s p=q\circ s . The formal definition of the r r -local covering is given in [ 4 , §4.2] . For this paper, we need only its equivalent characterization given by [ 4 , Lemmas 4.2, 4.3, and 4.4] . Given a connected graph G G and an integer r ⩾ 1 r\geqslant 1 , the r r -local covering p r : G r → G p_{r}:G_{r}\to G is the universal covering among the coverings of G G that preserve r / 2 r/2 -balls. The graph G r G_{r} is the r r -local cover of G G . Given G G and r ⩾ 1 r\geqslant 1 , both the r r -local covering p r : G r → G p_{r}:G_{r}\to G and the r r -local cover G r G_{r} are unique. 3. Proof of Theorem 1 In this section, we show that for r ⩾ 4 r\geqslant 4 , the r r -locally interval graphs are precisely the r r -acyclic circular-arc graphs, i.e. Conjecture 1.1 holds for r ⩾ 4 r\geqslant 4 . Cao, Grippo and Safe characterized the 3 3 -acyclic circular-arc graphs by forbidden induced subgraphs ( Theorem 3.1 ). They refer to 3 3 -acyclic circular-arc graphs as normal Helly circular-arc graphs . In the literature, 2 2 -acyclic circular-arc graphs are called normal . These types of circular-arc graphs have often been considered when being additionally Helly . By Lemma 2.1 , the 3 3 -acyclic circular-arc graphs are precisely the normal Helly circular-arc graphs. Theorem 3.1 (Cao, Grippo & Safe [ 3 ] , Theorem 1) . Let G G be a finite graph. Then the following are equivalent: (i) G G is 3 3 -acyclic (equivalently: normal Helly) circular-arc. (ii) G G is wheel-free (equivalently: 3 3 -locally chordal) and contains none of the graphs depicted in Figures 1 and 2 as an induced subgraph. (a) K 2 , 3 K_{2,3} (b) Twin- C 5 C^{5} (c) Domino (d) C 6 ¯ \bar{C^{6}} (e) FIS- 1 1 (f) FIS- 2 2 (g) Wheel W n W_{n} (h) C n ∗ C_{n}^{*} Figure 2 . Non-chordal minimal forbidden induced subgraphs of 3 3 -acyclic circularc arc graphs, n ⩾ 4 n\geqslant 4 . (a) Long Claw plus one edge (b) Long Claw plus two edges (c) Long Claw plus three edges Figure 3 . Additional forbidden induced subgraphs for r r -acyclic circular-arc graphs in the case r = 4 r=4 . We are now ready to prove Theorem 1 . Proof of Theorem 1 . We prove Item (ii) → \to Item (i) → \to Item (iii) → \to Item (ii) , Item (iii) → \to Item (iv) , and Item (iv) → \to Item (iii) . Claim 1 . Item (ii) implies Item (i) . Proof. By Item (ii) , the r r -local cover G r G_{r} is interval, and thus Lemma 2.4 yields that G r G_{r} is r r -locally interval. As p r p_{r} preserves r / 2 r/2 -balls, it follows that G G is r r -locally interval, as desired. ∎ Claim 2 . For every graph F F depicted in Figure 1 , there is a vertex v F v_{F} of F F such that F ⊆ B F ​ ( v F , 4 / 2 ) F\subseteq B_{F}(v_{F},4/2) . For every graph F F depicted in Figure 3 , there is a vertex v F v_{F} such that F ∩ B F ​ ( v F , 4 / 2 ) F\cap B_{F}(v_{F},4/2) is the long claw. Proof. In both cases, let v F v_{F} be the green vertex in the respective figure. The blue vertices are the ones with distance 2 2 to v F v_{F} . ∎ Claim 3 . Item (i) implies Item (iii) . Proof. Since G G is r r -locally interval, it holds that G G is r r -locally chordal. Thus, by Theorem 1.3 , G G is wheel-free and r r -chordal. Suppose for a contradiction that G G contains a graph from Figure 1 as an induced subgraph F F . Then, F F is contained in B F ​ ( v F , 4 / 2 ) ⊆ B G ​ ( v F , 4 / 2 ) B_{F}(v_{F},4/2)\subseteq B_{G}(v_{F},4/2) by Claim 2 . Since F F is an induced subgraph of G G and r ⩾ 4 r\geqslant 4 , the graph F F is an induced subgraph of the interval graph B G ​ ( v F , r / 2 ) B_{G}(v_{F},r/2) , which is a contradiction to Theorem 2.3 . This completes the proof of the claim for r ⩾ 5 r\geqslant 5 . Now assume r = 4 r=4 . Suppose for a contradiction that G G contains a graph from Figure 3 as an induced subgraph F F . Then, there is a vertex v F v_{F} of F F such that F ∩ B F ​ ( v F , 4 / 2 ) F\cap B_{F}(v_{F},4/2) is the long claw depicted in Figure 1 . Since F F is an induced subgraph of G G and v F v_{F} is a vertex of G G , it follows that F ∩ B F ​ ( v F , 4 / 2 ) = F ∩ B G ​ ( v F , 4 / 2 ) F\cap B_{F}(v_{F},4/2)=F\cap B_{G}(v_{F},4/2) , and thus the long claw is an induced subgraph of B G ​ ( v F , 4 / 2 ) B_{G}(v_{F},4/2) , which contradicts Theorem 2.3 . This completes the proof of the claim for r = 4 r=4 . ∎ Claim 4 . Item (iii) implies Item (ii) . Proof. By Theorem 2.3 , it suffices to show that the r r -local cover G r G_{r} has no graph F F depicted in Figure 1 as an induced subgraph. Suppose for a contradiction that G r G_{r} contains a graph depicted in Figure 1 as an induced subgraph F F . Then F F is contained in B F ​ ( v F , 4 / 2 ) ⊆ B G r ​ ( v F , 4 / 2 ) B_{F}(v_{F},4/2)\subseteq B_{G_{r}}(v_{F},4/2) by Claim 2 . First, assume that r ⩾ 5 r\geqslant 5 . Since F F is an induced subgraph of G r G_{r} and r ⩾ 5 r\geqslant 5 , the graph F F is an induced subgraph of the subgraph B G r ​ ( v F , 5 / 2 ) B_{G_{r}}(v_{F},5/2) of G r G_{r} . As p r p_{r} preserves r / 2 r/2 -balls and r ⩾ 5 r\geqslant 5 , the graph F F is also an induced subgraph of the induced subgraph B G ​ ( p r ​ ( v F ) , 5 / 2 ) B_{G}(p_{r}(v_{F}),5/2) of G G , so F F is an induced subgraph of G G , which contradicts that F F is not an induced subgraph of G G by Item (iii) . Now, assume that r = 4 r=4 . Since F F is an induced subgraph of G r G_{r} and r = 4 r=4 , the graph F F is an induced subgraph of the subgraph B G r ​ ( v F , 4 / 2 ) B_{G_{r}}(v_{F},4/2) of G r G_{r} . As p r p_{r} preserves r / 2 r/2 -balls and r = 4 r=4 , the graph F F is also an induced subgraph of B G ​ ( p r ​ ( v F ) , 4 / 2 ) B_{G}(p_{r}(v_{F}),4/2) . Then only the blue vertices of F F in Figure 1 have distance 2 2 to p r ​ ( v F ) p_{r}(v_{F}) in G G . If F F is not the long claw, then it is a simple check that every potential edge of G G between some pair of the blue vertices yields an induced cycle of length 4 4 in G G , which contradicts that G G is in particular 4 4 -chordal by Item (iii) . Thus assume that F F is the long claw. Then, the addition of the potential edge of G G between pairs of blue vertices in Figure 1a gives one of the two graphs depicted in Figure 3 , which then is an induced subgraph of G G , contradicting Item (iii) . ∎ Claim 5 . Item (iii) implies Item (iv) . Proof. First, we note that it suffices to show that G G has none of the graphs depicted in Figure 2 as induced subgraphs. Suppose G G forbids each graph in Figure 2 . By Item (iii) , G G forbids each graph in Figure 1 . Then G G is a 3-acyclic circular-arc graph by Theorem 3.1 . As G G is r r -locally chordal by Item (iii) and Theorem 1.3 , it follows that G G is in fact r r -acyclic circular-arc by Theorem 2.2 . Therefore, it is sufficient to show that G G forbids each graph in Figure 2 as an induced subgraph. Every graph F F depicted in Figure 2 except the one in 2f contains an induced cycle of length 4 4 , and thus is not an induced subgraph of G G by Item (iii) . It remains to show that the graph F F depicted in Figure 2f is not an induced subgraph of G G . Since F F contains an induced cycle of length 5, it follows from Item (iii) that G G excludes F F as an induced subgraph when r ⩾ 5 r\geqslant 5 . If r = 4 r=4 , then the induced subgraph F F of G G is precisely the graph depicted in Figure 3b , which is a contradiction to Item (iii) . ∎ Claim 6 . Item (iv) implies Item (iii) . Proof. By Theorem 3.1 and r ⩾ 4 > 3 r\geqslant 4>3 , an r r -acyclic circular-arc graph G G contains none of the graphs depicted in Figure 1 as induced subgraphs. This completes the proof for r ⩾ 5 r\geqslant 5 . For r = 4 r=4 , it suffices to observe that none of the graphs in Figure 3 is circular-arc. ∎ This completes the proof of Theorem 1 . ∎ 4. Characterization of 3-locally chordal graphs Now, we revisit the case of Conjecture 1.1 where r ⩽ 3 r\leqslant 3 . For r ⩽ 2 r\leqslant 2 , Conjecture 1.1 is trivially wrong: every graph is 0 0 -, 1 1 -, and 2 2 -locally interval, since the relevant balls are stars and stars are interval graphs, but there are graphs, e.g. the long claw depicted in Figure 1a , which are not circular-arc. For r = 3 r=3 , Conjecture 1.1 fails for the same reason: the long claw is still 3 3 -locally interval but not a circular-arc graph. However, every 3-acyclic circular-arc graph is 3-locally interval by definition. One may characterize 3 3 -locally interval graphs by forbidden induced subgraphs, as follows. A vertex v v in a graph G G is an apex vertex if v v is adjacent to every other vertex in G G . Theorem 2 . Let G G be a finite graph. Then the following are equivalent: (i) G G is 3 3 -locally interval. (ii) G G is wheel-free (equivalently: 3 3 -locally chordal) and contains none of the graphs depicted in Figure 1 with an apex vertex added as an induced subgraph. Proof. Every 3 3 -locally interval graph is 3 3 -locally chordal, as interval graphs are chordal. It is easy to check that each graph in Figure 1 with an apex vertex added is not 3-locally interval. Conversely, let G G be a 3 3 -locally chordal graph such that G G contains none of the graphs depicted in Figure 1 with an added apex vertex as induced subgraph. Consider a vertex v v of G G and the chordal 3 / 2 3/2 -ball B ≔ B G ​ ( v , 3 / 2 ) B\coloneqq B_{G}(v,3/2) around v v . By Theorem 2.3 , it suffices to show that B B contains none of the graphs depicted in Figure 1 as an induced subgraph. Suppose for a contradiction that B B contains a graph from Figure 1 as an induced subgraph B ′ B^{\prime} . Since v v is adjacent to all other vertices in B B but no vertex in the graphs depicted in Figure 1 is adjacent to all other vertices in that graph, v v is not a vertex of B ′ B^{\prime} . Thus, the subgraph induced on the vertex set of B ′ B^{\prime} together with v v is one of the induced subgraphs forbidden in G G . ∎ References [1] T. Abrishami, P. Knappe, and J. Kobler (2025) Locally chordal graphs . External Links: 2501.17320 Cited by: §1.1 , Theorem 1.3 . [2] T. Abrishami and P. Knappe The global structure of locally chordal graphs . Note: Preprint Cited by: §1.1 , Theorem 1.3 , §2.1 , Lemma 2.1 , Theorem 2.2 . [3] Y. Cao, L. N. Grippo, and M. D. Safe (2017) Forbidden induced subgraphs of normal helly circular-arc graphs: characterization and detection . Discrete Applied Mathematics 216 , pp. 67–83 . Cited by: §1 , Theorem 3.1 . [4] R. Diestel, R. W. Jacobs, P. Knappe, and J. Kurkofka (2022) Canonical graph decompositions via coverings . Note: To appear in Transactions of the AMS External Links: 2207.04855 Cited by: §1.1 , §2.3 . [5] R. Halin (1982) Some remarks on interval graphs . Combinatorica 2 ( 3 ), pp. 297–304 . Cited by: footnote 1 . [6] C. Lekkeikerker and J. Boland (1962) Representation of a finite graph by a set of intervals on the real line . Fundamenta Mathematicae 51 ( 1 ), pp. 45–64 . Cited by: §2.2 , Theorem 2.3 . Experimental support, please view the build logs for errors. Generated by L A T E xml . Instructions for reporting errors We are continuing to improve HTML versions of papers, and your feedback helps enhance accessibility and mobile support. To report errors in the HTML that will help us improve conversion and rendering, choose any of the methods listed below: Click the "Report Issue" ( ) button, located in the page header. Tip: You can select the relevant text first, to include it in your report. Our team has already identified the following issues . We appreciate your time reviewing and reporting rendering errors we may not have found yet. Your efforts will help us improve the HTML versions for all readers, because disability should not be a barrier to accessing research. Thank you